=== Q001
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Who is the PRIMARY audience for a company's published annual financial statements?
+ External users such as investors and lenders || Published accounts report to outsiders.
- The company's own production supervisors || They use management accounts.
- The marketing team only || Internal users.
- The company's auditors only || Auditors report on the accounts; they are not the main audience.
explain: Financial accounting reports on how directors have looked after the owners' resources (stewardship).
mistake: Thinking published accounts are prepared mainly for managers.
tip: Published = outsiders.
src: Study text ch1 - users (concept basis; original question)

=== Q002
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which user group needs the MOST detailed financial information?
+ Management || They plan, control and decide using detailed internal data.
- Shareholders || They see summarised published accounts.
- The tax authorities || They need taxable profit information.
- The general public || They need very little detail.
explain: Management relies on management accounts rather than published statements.
mistake: Choosing investors.
tip: Insiders need the most detail.
src: Study text ch1 - users (concept basis; original question)

=== Q003
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: A bank is deciding whether to renew a loan to a company. What is the bank MOST interested in?
+ Whether the company can repay the loan and interest when due || Lenders focus on repayment.
- The company's staff pension rates || Employees' interest.
- Continuity of supply of products || Customers' interest.
- The company's charitable donations || Public interest.
explain: Lenders look at cash flow, security and gearing.
mistake: Mixing up user needs.
tip: Lenders want their money back.
src: Study text ch1 - users (concept basis; original question)

=== Q004
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which type of business has a separate legal identity from its owners?
+ A limited liability company || Incorporation creates a separate legal person.
- A sole trader || Not legally separate.
- An ordinary partnership || Not legally separate.
- A sole trader with employees || Still not separate.
explain: The business entity concept treats all businesses as separate for accounting, but only companies are legally separate.
mistake: Confusing the accounting and legal views.
tip: Company = separate legal person.
src: Study text ch1 - entities (concept basis; original question)

=== Q005
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: What is the liability of a shareholder in a limited liability company?
+ Limited to the amount they have invested in shares || They cannot lose more.
- Unlimited || Applies to sole traders and partners.
- Joint and several for all company debts || Partnerships.
- Limited to their salary || Not related.
explain: This protection is a major advantage of incorporation.
mistake: Thinking shareholders are personally liable.
tip: Limited = capped at investment.
src: Study text ch1 - limited liability (concept basis; original question)

=== Q006
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What are the TWO fundamental qualitative characteristics in the Conceptual Framework?
+ Relevance and faithful representation || The two fundamentals.
- Comparability and timeliness || Enhancing characteristics.
- Understandability and verifiability || Enhancing characteristics.
- Prudence and consistency || Not the fundamentals.
explain: The four enhancing characteristics make useful information more useful.
mistake: Listing enhancing characteristics.
tip: Relevant + faithful.
src: Study text ch1 - qualitative characteristics (concept basis; original question)

=== Q007
type: num
level: F
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A business has assets of $50,000 and liabilities of $18,000. What is its equity ($)?
answer: 32000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Equity = assets - liabilities
step: Assets = 50,000; liabilities = 18,000.
step: Equity = 50,000 - 18,000 = $32,000.
check: 50000-18000
explain: Equity is the owners' residual interest.
mistake: Adding liabilities.
tip: A - L = E.
src: Study text ch1 - accounting equation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q008
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Which item is a CURRENT asset of a furniture retailer?
+ Amounts owed by credit customers || Expected to be received within 12 months.
- The shop building || Non-current.
- Delivery vans || Non-current.
- A 5-year bank loan || A liability.
explain: Current assets are expected to be realised within the operating cycle or 12 months.
mistake: Classifying vans as current.
tip: Turns into cash soon = current.
src: Study text ch1 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q009
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: Which statement shows a company's assets, liabilities and equity at the reporting date?
+ The statement of financial position || A snapshot at a point in time.
- The statement of profit or loss || Covers a period.
- The statement of cash flows || Cash movements.
- The statement of changes in equity || Equity movements.
explain: The other statements cover the whole period.
mistake: Confusing position with performance.
tip: Position = one date.
src: Study text ch1 - components (concept basis; original question)

=== Q010
type: mcq
level: F
cat: concept
topic: t6
status: review
q: What does the going concern concept assume?
+ The business will keep operating for the foreseeable future || No intention or need to close.
- The business will close within a year || The opposite.
- All assets are valued at sale value || Not the assumption.
- Profits will rise each year || Not the assumption.
explain: If a business is not a going concern, assets may be shown at break-up values.
mistake: Linking going concern to profitability.
tip: Will it keep going?
src: Study text ch1 - going concern (concept basis; original question)

=== Q011
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t6
status: review
q: A company sells goods to a bank and agrees to buy them back in six months at a higher price. Economically this is a loan. Which concept requires it to be shown as a loan?
+ Substance over form || Report economic reality, not legal form.
- Going concern || Not relevant.
- Materiality || Not relevant.
- Business entity || Not relevant.
explain: The "sale" is really borrowing secured on the goods.
mistake: Following legal form.
tip: What really happened?
src: Study text ch1 - substance over form (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q012
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which is an ENHANCING qualitative characteristic?
+ Comparability || Helps users compare entities and periods.
- Relevance || Fundamental.
- Faithful representation || Fundamental.
- Materiality || An aspect of relevance.
explain: Enhancing: comparability, verifiability, timeliness, understandability.
mistake: Listing a fundamental characteristic.
tip: Remember the four enhancers.
src: Study text ch1 - characteristics (concept basis; original question)

=== Q013
type: mr
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO are components of faithful representation?
+ Complete || All necessary information included.
+ Neutral || Without bias.
- Timely || An enhancing characteristic.
- Predictive value || An aspect of relevance.
explain: The third component is being free from error.
mistake: Mixing relevance and faithful representation.
tip: Complete, neutral, free from error.
src: Study text ch1 - faithful representation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q014
type: match
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Match each feature with the type of business.
pair: Unlimited liability of a single owner => Sole trader
pair: Capital and current accounts for each owner => Partnership
pair: Managed by a board of directors => Limited liability company
pair: Owners receive dividends, not drawings => Limited liability company
explain: Company owners are shareholders; sole traders and partners take drawings.
mistake: Calling dividends drawings.
tip: Shareholders get dividends.
src: Study text ch1 - entities (concept basis; original question)

=== Q015
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A sole trader's capital at the start of the year was $20,000. Profit for the year was $8,000 and drawings were $5,000. What is closing capital ($)?
answer: 23000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Closing capital = opening capital + profit - drawings
step: 20,000 + 8,000 = 28,000.
step: 28,000 - 5,000 = $23,000.
check: 20000+8000-5000
explain: Drawings reduce the owner's interest in the business.
mistake: Treating drawings as an expense.
tip: Profit adds; drawings subtract.
src: Study text ch1 - capital (concept basis; original question)

=== Q016
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: Which BEST describes a liability?
+ A present obligation to give up economic resources, arising from past events || The key features.
- A possible future expense || Must be present and from a past event.
- Any amount the owner has invested || That is equity.
- A resource controlled by the entity || That is an asset.
explain: An asset is a present economic resource controlled because of past events.
mistake: Including future possibilities.
tip: Present obligation + past event.
src: Study text ch1 - elements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q017
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t6
status: review
q: When is an item MATERIAL?
+ When omitting or misstating it could change users' decisions || A matter of judgement.
- Only when it exceeds $1 million || No fixed amount.
- Whenever it relates to cash || Not the test.
- Only when it is a non-current asset || Not the test.
explain: Materiality depends on size and nature.
mistake: Thinking materiality is a fixed number.
tip: Would it change a decision?
src: Study text ch1 - materiality (concept basis; original question)

=== Q018
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t6
status: review
q: The owner of a shop pays her personal holiday from the business bank account. Which concept means this must be treated as drawings?
+ Business entity || The business is separate from the owner.
- Going concern || Not relevant.
- Materiality || Not relevant.
- Accruals || Not relevant.
explain: Personal spending is not a business expense.
mistake: Recording it as an expense.
tip: Personal = drawings.
src: Study text ch1 - business entity (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q019
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: Which statement shows movements in share capital, revaluation surplus and retained earnings?
+ Statement of changes in equity || Equity movements.
- Statement of financial position || Closing balances only.
- Statement of cash flows || Cash only.
- Notes to the accounts || Supporting detail.
explain: Dividends paid appear here, not in profit or loss.
mistake: Putting dividends in the P/L.
tip: Equity changes = SOCE.
src: Study text ch1 - components (concept basis; original question)

=== Q020
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What is the MAIN purpose of the Conceptual Framework?
+ To help the IASB develop consistent standards and help preparers where no standard applies || Underlying principles.
- To replace all IFRSs || It does not override standards.
- To set tax rules || Not its role.
- To audit financial statements || Not its role.
explain: It also helps users understand standards.
mistake: Thinking it overrides standards.
tip: Framework = foundations.
src: Study text ch1 - Conceptual Framework (concept basis; original question)

=== Q021
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: Which is a feature of MANAGEMENT accounting rather than financial accounting?
+ Detailed, forward-looking information for internal planning and decisions || Internal focus.
- An annual report for shareholders || Financial accounting.
- Prepared under IFRS for external users || Financial accounting.
- Published for the public || Financial accounting.
explain: Management accounts need not follow accounting standards.
mistake: Mixing the two.
tip: Internal + future = management.
src: Study text ch1 - comparison (concept basis; original question)

=== Q022
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: How should a bank loan repayable in two years' time be classified at the reporting date?
+ Non-current liability || Due after more than 12 months.
- Current liability || Not due within 12 months.
- Non-current asset || It is owed.
- Equity || Not owners' funds.
explain: The portion due within 12 months would be current.
mistake: Calling all loans current.
tip: Over 12 months = non-current.
src: Study text ch1 - classification (concept basis; original question)

=== Q023
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What does the prudence principle require when judgements are made under uncertainty?
+ Caution, so that assets and income are not overstated and liabilities and expenses not understated || Supports neutrality.
- Deliberately understating profit || Not allowed.
- Always choosing the highest asset value || Not prudent.
- Ignoring uncertain items completely || Not required.
explain: Prudence must not create hidden reserves.
mistake: Thinking prudence means understating profit.
tip: Caution, not bias.
src: Study text ch1 - prudence (concept basis; original question)

=== Q024
type: num
level: S
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A company has equity of $75,000 and liabilities of $40,000. What are its total assets ($)?
answer: 115000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Assets = equity + liabilities
step: Equity = 75,000; liabilities = 40,000.
step: Assets = 75,000 + 40,000 = $115,000.
check: 75000+40000
explain: The accounting equation always balances.
mistake: Subtracting liabilities.
tip: A = E + L.
src: Study text ch1 - accounting equation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q025
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: Which is a DISADVANTAGE of operating as a limited liability company?
+ Accounts must be published and are subject to more regulation || Loss of privacy, more cost.
- Owners have unlimited liability || Liability is limited.
- Shares cannot be transferred || They can.
- It cannot own property || It can.
explain: Formation costs and audit requirements are other disadvantages.
mistake: Listing an advantage.
tip: Protection costs privacy.
src: Study text ch1 - incorporation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q026
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Information that helps users predict future cash flows has which quality?
+ Predictive value, part of relevance || Relevance includes predictive and confirmatory value.
- Verifiability || An enhancing characteristic.
- Neutrality || Faithful representation.
- Timeliness || An enhancing characteristic.
explain: Confirmatory value checks past predictions.
mistake: Calling it faithful representation.
tip: Predict = relevance.
src: Study text ch1 - relevance (concept basis; original question)

=== Q027
type: order
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: Put these steps in the order in which a full set of year-end statements is usually built up.
item: Statement of profit or loss and other comprehensive income
item: Statement of changes in equity
item: Statement of financial position
item: Statement of cash flows
explain: Profit feeds equity, which feeds the SOFP; cash flows draw on both.
mistake: Starting with the SOFP.
tip: Performance, equity, position, cash.
src: Study text ch1 - components (concept basis; original question)

=== Q028
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
q: How is INCOME defined in the Conceptual Framework?
+ Increases in assets or decreases in liabilities that increase equity, other than contributions from owners || Excludes capital introduced.
- Any cash received || Includes loans and capital.
- Money introduced by the owner || A contribution, not income.
- Decreases in assets || That is an expense effect.
explain: Expenses are the mirror image, excluding distributions to owners.
mistake: Treating capital introduced as income.
tip: Equity up, not from owners.
src: Study text ch1 - income (concept basis; original question)

=== Q029
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t6
status: review
q: A company buys a $15 stapler expected to last five years and expenses it immediately. Which concept justifies this?
+ Materiality || The amount is too small to affect decisions.
- Going concern || Not relevant.
- Substance over form || Not relevant.
- Business entity || Not relevant.
explain: Capitalising trivial items adds cost without benefit.
mistake: Insisting all long-lived items be capitalised.
tip: Immaterial = expense.
src: Study text ch1 - materiality (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q030
type: mcq
level: S
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What are employees and trade unions MOST interested in?
+ The stability and profitability of the employer, affecting job security and pay || Their livelihoods.
- Dividend yield || Investors.
- Loan security || Lenders.
- Tax payable || Government.
explain: Each user group has different needs.
mistake: Mixing groups.
tip: Jobs and pay.
src: Study text ch1 - users (concept basis; original question)

=== Q031
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t3
status: review
q: A company changes its depreciation method every year to smooth profits. Which qualitative characteristic is MOST damaged?
+ Comparability || Users cannot compare periods.
- Timeliness || Not affected.
- Verifiability || Less directly.
- Understandability || Less directly.
explain: Consistent policies support comparability.
mistake: Ignoring the effect of policy changes.
tip: Changing methods = harder to compare.
src: Study text ch1 - comparability (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q032
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t6
status: review
q: A company leases a machine for 10 years, which is its entire useful life. Legally the lessor owns it. How does substance over form affect the accounts?
+ The company controls the machine's economic benefits, so it recognises an asset and a liability || Reflect economic reality.
- The machine is ignored because the company does not own it || Ignores substance.
- Only lease payments are expensed, with nothing recognised || Ignores control.
- The lessor records nothing || Not the point.
explain: Control, not legal title, drives recognition.
mistake: Following legal form.
tip: Control = asset.
src: Study text ch1 - substance over form (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q033
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: A sole trader starts the year with capital of $42,000. During the year she introduces $6,000 of extra capital, makes a profit of $15,500 and takes drawings of $11,200.
q: What is her closing capital ($)?
answer: 52300
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Closing capital = opening + capital introduced + profit - drawings
step: 42,000 + 6,000 + 15,500 = 63,500.
step: 63,500 - 11,200 = $52,300.
check: 42000+6000+15500-11200
explain: Capital introduced is not income.
mistake: Treating capital introduced as profit.
tip: Owner in/out vs profit.
src: Study text ch1 - capital (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q034
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: A business's net assets rose from $30,000 to $38,000 over the year. The owner introduced $5,000 of capital and took drawings of $9,000. What was the profit for the year ($)?
answer: 12000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Profit = increase in net assets - capital introduced + drawings
step: Increase in net assets = 38,000 - 30,000 = 8,000.
step: Profit = 8,000 - 5,000 + 9,000 = $12,000.
check: (38000-30000)-5000+9000
explain: Changes in net assets come from profit and owner transactions.
mistake: Reporting the $8,000 increase as profit.
tip: Strip out owner transactions.
src: Study text ch1 - accounting equation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q035
type: mr
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t4
status: review
select: 2
q: Which TWO items are assets of a trading company?
+ Inventory held for resale || Controlled resource.
+ Prepaid insurance for next year || Future benefit controlled.
- An overdraft || A liability.
- Share capital || Equity.
explain: Assets are resources controlled as a result of past events.
mistake: Calling the overdraft an asset.
tip: Controlled resource = asset.
src: Study text ch1 - elements (concept basis; original question)

=== Q036
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t6
status: review
q: A trainee says the business entity concept means sole traders have limited liability. What is wrong?
+ The concept is an accounting convention only; legally, a sole trader remains personally liable || Accounting separation ≠ legal separation.
- Nothing is wrong || The law does not separate them.
- Sole traders have no liability || Unlimited.
- The concept applies only to companies || It applies to all businesses.
explain: Only incorporation gives limited liability.
mistake: Confusing accounting and law.
tip: Separate books, not separate person.
src: Study text ch1 - business entity (concept basis; original question)

=== Q037
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: A company publishes its annual accounts 14 months after the year end. Which enhancing characteristic is weakest?
+ Timeliness || Late information loses usefulness.
- Comparability || Not mainly.
- Verifiability || Not mainly.
- Neutrality || A faithful representation component.
explain: Information must be available in time to influence decisions.
mistake: Choosing understandability.
tip: Late = not timely.
src: Study text ch1 - timeliness (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q038
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t6
status: review
q: A manufacturer is about to close because its main customer has collapsed and no buyer can be found. What effect does this have on the accounts?
+ The going concern basis may not be appropriate, and assets may need to be shown at break-up values || The basis changes.
- No effect - always use going concern || Not when closure is likely.
- Assets should be revalued upwards || Unlikely.
- Liabilities should be removed || They remain.
explain: Directors must assess going concern at each year end.
mistake: Ignoring going concern doubts.
tip: Closing down = break-up basis.
src: Study text ch1 - going concern (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q039
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t1
status: review
q: What does "stewardship" mean in the context of financial accounts?
+ Reporting on how the directors have managed the resources entrusted to them by the owners || Accountability.
- Forecasting next year's profit || Not stewardship.
- Setting product prices || Not stewardship.
- Paying taxes || Not stewardship.
explain: Financial statements hold directors to account.
mistake: Confusing with planning.
tip: Look after the owners' resources.
src: Study text ch1 - stewardship (concept basis; original question)

=== Q040
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Which characteristic means that different knowledgeable, independent observers would agree that a figure is a faithful representation?
+ Verifiability || Agreement among independent observers.
- Timeliness || Not this.
- Comparability || Not this.
- Relevance || Fundamental, not this.
explain: Verifiability can be direct or indirect.
mistake: Calling this neutrality.
tip: Could others check it?
src: Study text ch1 - verifiability (concept basis; original question)

=== Q041
type: match
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: Match each enhancing characteristic with its description.
pair: Comparability => Users can identify similarities and differences between items
pair: Verifiability => Independent observers could agree the figures
pair: Timeliness => Available in time to influence decisions
pair: Understandability => Classified and presented clearly and concisely
explain: These enhance information that is already relevant and faithfully represented.
mistake: Mixing definitions.
tip: Compare, check, on time, clear.
src: Study text ch1 - enhancing characteristics (concept basis; original question)

=== Q042
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
scenario: A trader's business has: premises $60,000; van $12,000; inventory $8,500; receivables $4,300; bank $2,200; payables $6,800; loan (due in 3 years) $20,000.
q: What is the owner's capital ($)?
answer: 60200
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Capital = total assets - total liabilities
step: Assets = 60,000 + 12,000 + 8,500 + 4,300 + 2,200 = 87,000.
step: Liabilities = 6,800 + 20,000 = 26,800; capital = 87,000 - 26,800 = $60,200.
check: 60000+12000+8500+4300+2200-6800-20000
explain: The equation holds whatever the type of liability.
mistake: Leaving out the long-term loan.
tip: All assets less all liabilities.
src: Study text ch1 - accounting equation (concept basis; original figures)

=== Q043
type: mcq
level: C
cat: trap
topic: t4
status: review
q: A trainee records $10,000 cash introduced by the owner as revenue. What is the effect?
+ Profit is overstated by $10,000; it should be credited to capital || Owner contributions are not income.
- No effect || Profit is wrong.
- Profit is understated || Overstated.
- Assets are overstated || Cash was received; assets are right.
explain: Income excludes contributions from equity holders.
mistake: Treating capital as income.
tip: Owner's money = capital.
src: Study text ch1 - income (concept basis; original question)

=== Q044
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t2
status: review
q: How does a partnership record each partner's share of profit and drawings?
+ In separate capital and current accounts for each partner || Tracks each partner's interest.
- In a single share capital account || Companies.
- As dividends || Companies.
- As expenses in profit or loss || Not expenses.
explain: Partners' liability is usually unlimited, joint and several.
mistake: Using company terminology.
tip: Partners have current accounts.
src: Study text ch1 - partnerships (concept basis; original question)

=== Q045
type: mcq
level: C
cat: scenario
topic: t6
status: review
q: A small error of $400 is found in a company with profit of $5 million. However, the error relates to an undisclosed payment to a director. Why might it still be material?
+ Materiality depends on nature as well as size; related-party payments can matter to users || Nature matters.
- It is not material because it is small || Ignores nature.
- All errors are material || Not true.
- Directors' payments are never disclosed || They are.
explain: Users may care about governance issues regardless of amount.
mistake: Judging materiality on size alone.
tip: Size AND nature.
src: Study text ch1 - materiality (concept basis; original scenario)

=== Q046
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t5
status: review
q: Where would a reader find the accounting policies a company has used?
+ In the notes to the financial statements || Policies and disclosures.
- In the statement of cash flows || Not there.
- In the statement of changes in equity || Not there.
- They are never disclosed || They must be.
explain: Notes explain and expand on the main statements.
mistake: Thinking policies are optional.
tip: Notes = policies.
src: Study text ch1 - notes (concept basis; original question)

=== Q047
type: num
level: C
cat: calc
topic: t4
status: review
q: During a year a company's assets increased by $25,000 and its liabilities increased by $9,000. No shares were issued and no dividends paid. What was the profit ($)?
answer: 16000
tol: 0
unit: $
formula: Change in equity = change in assets - change in liabilities
step: Increase in equity = 25,000 - 9,000 = 16,000.
step: With no owner transactions, profit = $16,000.
check: 25000-9000
explain: Profit is the only source of the equity change here.
mistake: Adding the liability increase.
tip: Equity change = profit (if no owner flows).
src: Study text ch1 - accounting equation (concept basis; original question)

=== Q048
type: mcq
level: C
cat: concept
topic: t3
status: review
q: What is the objective of general purpose financial reporting?
+ To provide financial information useful to investors, lenders and other creditors in making decisions about providing resources || Decision usefulness.
- To calculate tax payable || A secondary use.
- To value the business for sale || Not the objective.
- To help managers set prices || Management accounting.
explain: Primary users are present and potential providers of capital.
mistake: Naming management as the primary user.
tip: Useful for resource decisions.
src: Study text ch1 - objective (concept basis; original question)

=== Q049
type: mcq
level: F
cat: revision
topic: t3
status: review
important: 1
q: Which list correctly separates the qualitative characteristics?
+ Fundamental: relevance, faithful representation. Enhancing: comparability, verifiability, timeliness, understandability || Core classification.
- Fundamental: comparability, timeliness. Enhancing: relevance, faithful representation || Reversed.
- All six are fundamental || Two levels.
- Fundamental: prudence, consistency || Not the framework list.
explain: Enhancing characteristics cannot make irrelevant information useful.
mistake: Reversing the groups.
tip: 2 fundamental + 4 enhancing.
src: Study text ch1 - characteristics (concept basis; original question)

=== Q050
type: mcq
level: S
cat: revision
topic: t4
status: review
important: 1
q: Which equation is always true?
+ Assets = liabilities + equity || The accounting equation.
- Assets = liabilities - equity || Wrong sign.
- Equity = assets + liabilities || Wrong sign.
- Liabilities = assets + equity || Wrong.
explain: Every transaction keeps the equation in balance.
mistake: Mixing signs.
tip: A = L + E.
src: Study text ch1 - accounting equation (concept basis; original question)
